From 19d98e1574ee8a83cb04e778503e13fe4dbc96fb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 21:30:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] tools/dvbridge: the fidelity step-down of the composer of
 ETSI GS CCM 001

The levels of dvbridge_render_set_step() set the upscalers of the planes
(libplacebo-9920): the chroma of the base layer, then the chroma, then
the luma of the enhancement layer, scaled up bilinearly. The composer of
ETSI GS CCM 001, which the bridge asks for by default, uses none of the
last two: it resamples the enhancement layer with the integer filters of
annex B.3, and its chroma is the composed one. With it, levels 2 and 3
drew the picture of level 1, and level 1 was the most costly in
fidelity of what could be lowered (up to 331 codes of 4095 on picture
51 of the FEL test stream, at edges of strong colour).

step_down() now sets the ladder of both composers, and libplacebo takes
that of the one that composes the picture (the composer of the clause
composes by the reshaping a picture the clause does not cover). The
composer's, from libplacebo-9940, ordered by what each rung costs in
fidelity for the texture fetches it saves, measured on a software
renderer against the faithful picture (64-bit floats and the GPU, which
agree within one code at every level) and counted in the shaders:
- level 1: the luma of the enhancement layer between columns from the
  two nearest samples in place of 8 taps: 25 of about 197 million
  fetches of a 3840x2160 frame; on picture 51, 11.7 % of the pixels of
  the active picture change, by 9 codes at most (mean 0.17), the best
  ratio of the rungs that keep every pixel within a few codes;
- level 2: also between rows (4 taps: 8 million) and its chroma between
  columns (6 million), which save too little to be levels of their own
  and cost more for what they save (mean 0.14 and 0.27 for 8 and 6
  million): 34.3 %, 12 codes at most;
- level 3: also the composed chroma scaled up bilinearly, a pass fewer
  and 66 million fetches more: 47.8 %, up to 331 codes; on average the
  cheapest for what it saves, but the only rung with a worst case that
  shows, so the last.
Levels 1 and 2 change nothing where the enhancement layer is flat
(pictures 3 and 27); level 3 changes 2.1 % and 11.5 % of those, by 6
and 7 codes at most. dvbridge_render_step_levels() gives 0x9 without
the residual of an enhancement layer when the composer of the clause is
in use (only level 3 changes anything then), 0xf with it, and for the
reshaping what it gave. The log says what a level changes for the
composer in use (dvbridge_render_ccm_composer()), and the help of
videoscreen.hadesdvstepdown, which said that no pixel moves by more than
16 codes, now says that the last step can move a few further (English,
and the three Spanish files of Estuary).

Measured on the integrated tree with libplacebo-9930, 9931, 9939 and
9940: the render test of the bridge passes ("3 24 8", "3 24 6" and
"half", the render test and its copy merged with the bridge's own checks; with the
reshaping too), with its two ways byte for byte the same in every
drawing; the host test of CDVStepDown passes, also with this ladder as
the bridge now gives it.
